atters, including its overall alternation rate or its heads/tails balance.\n\n**Why this one:** The brief asked for something simpler or riskier than the current set. The critique of the best model, `personal_ideal_alternation`, found that people penalise long runs more than that model predicts (it survives the false-discovery correction, q ≈ 0.008). This candidate keeps the best model's per-person ideal but judges by the longest streak instead of the switching rate. The two features come apart on pairs like `HHTHTHTT` versus `HTHHTTHT`, so it should pass the novelty check. If streaks alone are the wrong cue, the model comparison should show that clearly.\n\n**How it's built:** It copies the structure of the incumbent. The streak feature is scaled from 0 (strict alternation) to 1 (a single run). Each person's ideal comes from a population distribution, parameterised so the sampler handles it well. The choice depends on the difference in squared distance to that ideal, with one sensitivity parameter.\n\n**Self-check result:** It completed the 100-draw smoke fit with a finite ELPD-LOO of −1063.9. Two things are still unknown:\n- **Reliability:** On the smoke fit, 7.5% of trials had high Pareto-k values, well above the 1% tolerance, so that ELPD estimate isn't reliable. This may or may not persist in the full four-chain fit. If it does, the model can still be admitted but can't be exported as the winner.\n- **Not checked:** Convergence can't be verified from a single chain, and neither novelty against the other models nor fit time was tested. The smoke fit took about 36 seconds.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_2/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`","ttft_ms":10035,"type":"result","duration_ms":308666,"uuid":"0793212b-a703-44fd-8b48-0da8ffc13d55","ttft_stream_ms":3327,"time_to_request_ms":115,"first_content_frame_ms":3328,"queued_turn_count"